---
title: Magnolia Bakery's Banana Pudding
description: Layered banana pudding set with instant vanilla mix and whipped cream, chilled until the wafers go soft.
tags: [pudding, make-ahead]
course: dessert
difficulty: easy
author: Magnolia Bakery
source:
  name: NYT Cooking
  author: Priya Krishna
  url: https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1023785-magnolia-bakerys-banana-pudding
servings: 12-16
time: 5h30m
---

In the bowl of a #stand mixer{} fitted with the whisk attachment, beat the @sweetened condensed milk{1%can}(14 oz) and @ice-cold water{1 1/2%cup} on low speed until blended, then increase the speed to medium and whisk until well combined, about ~whisk{1%minute}.

Add the @instant vanilla pudding mix{1%package}(3.4 oz) and beat until there are no lumps and the mixture is smooth, about ~beat{2%minutes}.

Transfer the mixture to a #medium bowl{}, cover and refrigerate until firm, at least ~chill{1%hour} or overnight.

Using the stand mixer with the whisk attachment, whip the @cold heavy cream{3%cup} on medium speed for about ~whip{1%minute}, until the cream starts to thicken, then increase the speed to medium-high and whip until stiff peaks form. Be careful not to overwhip.

With the mixer running on low speed, add the pudding mixture a spoonful at a time. Mix until well blended and no streaks of pudding remain.

To assemble, set aside 4 to 5 of the @vanilla wafer cookies{1%box}(11 oz, such as Nilla), whole or crumbled, for garnish.

In a 4- to 5-quart #trifle bowl{} or a wide glass bowl (or in 12 8-ounce bowls or ramekins), spread one-quarter of the pudding over the bottom and layer with one-third of the cookies and one-third of the @ripe bananas{4-5}(sliced), enough to cover the layer. Repeat the layering twice more. End with a final layer of pudding.

Cover tightly with #plastic wrap{} and refrigerate for ~chill{4-6%hours}. The cookies should be tender when poked with a knife.

> This dessert is best served within 12 hours of assembling.

To serve, garnish the top with the reserved cookies or cookie crumbs.
